CH1_BACKGROUND = [
    ("h3", "Identity verification, authentication, authorization and session management"),
    ("p", "Four activities are often grouped under the word authentication, and separating them clarifies what Ninsho does and does not do. *Identity verification* establishes that a real person or organization corresponds to an account, for example by checking a document or an email address. *Authentication* establishes that the party making a request is the holder of an account, typically by a password, a passkey or a federated sign-in. *Authorization* decides whether that authenticated party may perform the requested operation on the requested resource. *Session management* covers everything between a successful authentication and the end of the session: issuing a credential that stands in for the authentication, checking it on later requests, renewing it, and withdrawing it at logout or when it is suspected to be stolen."),
    ("p", "Ninsho deliberately implements only the last two. The host application verifies passwords or passkey assertions and owns the user records; once it is satisfied, it calls `createSession()` with a *principal* (a user identifier, roles, scopes and an optional tenant) and Ninsho takes over the session lifecycle and the route-level authorization checks. The optional browser client handles how the browser holds and presents credentials, and the optional WebAuthn package assists the application with passkey authentication without taking over the user records. Identity verification is outside the scope of this project."),
    ("h3", "Access tokens, refresh tokens and their lifetimes"),
    ("p", "An *access token* accompanies every request to a protected route and should be short-lived, because it is the credential most often exposed in logs, browser memory and network traces. A *refresh token* is presented only to a refresh endpoint and is used to obtain a new access token when the current one expires, so that the user does not have to authenticate again every few minutes. In Ninsho the defaults are an access-token lifetime of 300 seconds and a refresh-token lifetime of 604,800 seconds (seven days). The refresh lifetime is also the absolute lifetime of the *token family*: every refresh token produced by rotation inherits the family's fixed expiry, so a session cannot be extended indefinitely by refreshing it."),
    ("p", "Each refresh token is single-use. Presenting it consumes it and returns a replacement with the next *generation* number; the old token is recorded as consumed by a short-lived *tombstone*. This rotation is what allows a stolen refresh token to be detected: if both the legitimate client and an attacker hold the same token, the second presentation finds the tombstone and reveals the theft [4]."),
    ("h3", "Practical problems in session lifecycle management"),
    ("p", "The difficulties that motivated Ninsho are not exotic cryptographic attacks but ordinary operational situations in which a straightforward implementation behaves incorrectly. Table {T:problems} lists the situations that recur in this report, the consequence of handling each naively, and the mechanism Ninsho uses. Each mechanism is described in Chapter 3 and its evidence is reported in Chapter 4."),
    ("tbl", "problems", "Session-management problems and the corresponding Ninsho mechanisms",
     ["Situation", "Naive consequence", "Ninsho mechanism"],
     [["User logs out while a copy of the access token exists elsewhere", "Copied token keeps working until expiry", "Opaque record deleted (or PASETO deny-list entry written) at logout; next verification fails"],
      ["Several application instances behind a load balancer", "Logout on one instance not visible to another", "All state in a shared store; every instance reads the same record"],
      ["Refresh token stolen and used by an attacker", "Attacker silently holds a renewable session", "Single-use rotation; replay after the grace window revokes the whole family and emits refresh.reuse_detected"],
      ["Two browser tabs refresh at the same moment", "One tab is signed out or reuse is falsely detected", "Atomic consume (one winner) and a 30-second grace mapping that hands the loser the same replacement"],
      ["Bearer access token copied from a log or proxy", "Copy is usable by anyone", "Optional DPoP binding: every request needs a fresh proof signed by the client's key"],
      ["Captured DPoP proof replayed", "Proof reused within its acceptance window", "Proof identifier claimed once with an atomic set-if-absent"],
      ["Store unreachable during a request", "Revocation silently unchecked", "Fail closed by default: HTTP 503 instead of an unverified pass"],
      ["Password changed or account compromised", "Old sessions survive", "revokeAllForUser() over the user's session index"],
      ["Signed-in user changes an identifier in the URL", "Reads another user's data (BOLA)", "requireOwner() refuses when the owner cannot be established or differs"],
      ["Credential guessing against the login route", "Unlimited attempts", "Two-dimensional rate limiting by address and account"]],
     [3, 3, 5]),
]

