# ── Chapter 2 ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
CH2_REVIEW = [
    ("h3", "Server-side sessions, opaque tokens and introspection"),
    ("p", "The oldest design keeps all session state on the server and gives the client only a random identifier, usually in a cookie. The OAuth 2.0 framework [13] leaves the format of access tokens to the authorization server, and OAuth token introspection {R:rfc7662} standardizes how a resource server can ask the issuer whether an opaque token is active. The cost of this design is a lookup on every request; the benefit is that revocation, expiry and the token's meaning are all decided by the server at the moment of use. Ninsho's default *opaque* strategy follows this design but performs the lookup directly against the shared store rather than through an introspection endpoint, and it stores only a SHA-256 hash of each token, so that read access to the store does not yield usable credentials."),
    ("p", "Calzavara et al. {R:calzavara} surveyed attacks on web sessions, including session hijacking and fixation, and the defences proposed for them, and compared those defences on protection, usability, compatibility and ease of deployment. Their survey frames the session as a sequence of states that an attacker may try to enter, which is the view adopted in Chapter 3's lifecycle design."),
    ("h3", "PASETO and the removal of algorithm agility"),
    ("p", "Many JWT vulnerabilities stem from the token itself naming the algorithm used to verify it, which allows an attacker to choose a weaker or different algorithm [9]. PASETO version 4 removes this negotiation: the version and purpose prefix `v4.public.` fixes the algorithm to Ed25519 [18], and the header, payload, footer and an optional implicit assertion are bound together by pre-authentication encoding before signing [14]. Ninsho's optional PASETO strategy implements v4.public with a key identifier in the footer, requires issuer and audience, and checks the official test vectors. A signed token can be verified without the store, which is useful when several services verify independently, but revocation then needs a deny-list held in the store, which reintroduces the lookup it was meant to avoid."),
    ("h3", "Revocation strategies and their consistency trade-offs"),
    ("p", "OAuth token revocation {R:rfc7009} defines how a client tells the authorization server that a token is no longer needed; it does not define how every resource server learns of the revocation. The practical strategies differ in how quickly a revocation takes effect, what state they require and how they fail when that state is lost. Relying on a short lifetime alone needs no state but leaves a revoked token usable until it expires. A deny-list of revoked identifiers takes effect immediately but must be kept until each token expires, and if it is lost the revoked tokens become valid again. A per-user version number carried in each token revokes all of a user's tokens with one counter but cannot revoke a single session. Introspection asks the issuer on every request and fails closed if the issuer is unreachable. A reference (opaque) token is itself the lookup key, so revocation is deletion and losing the store ends every session. Ninsho's default uses the last of these and its PASETO strategy the deny-list; the difference in failure behaviour is the property this project examines most closely in Chapter 4."),
    ("h3", "Session hijacking and consistency across tabs and devices"),
    ("p", "Sivakorn et al. {R:sivakorn} showed that session cookies sent over unencrypted connections allowed attackers to hijack accounts on major services and, in some cases, to bypass additional authentication requirements. Squarcina et al. [11] later showed that even with modern cookie attributes, weaknesses in how cookies are scoped and overwritten allow session integrity attacks. Both results argue for limiting what a stolen credential can do, which is the purpose of sender-constraining with DPoP [5], and for keeping the long-lived credential in an HTTP-only cookie rather than in script-readable storage."),
    ("p", "Consistency across browser tabs is a client-side problem that the reviewed specifications do not address. When one tab logs out, other tabs of the same origin may still hold an access token in memory and may be in the middle of a refresh. Ninsho's client uses the BroadcastChannel API to send credential-free notices so that peer tabs discard their in-memory state; the server remains authoritative, and a tab that misses a notice learns of the logout on its next request."),
]

